	<key>CADisableMinimumFrameDurationOnPhone</key>
	<true/>
	<!--
	  Without this key the generated plist defaults CFBundleName to
	  $(PRODUCT_NAME), and App Store delivery rejects names that are
	  already taken by another app (ITMS-90129). Keep it set to the
	  app's display name; it must be unique on the App Store.
	-->
	<key>CFBundleName</key>
	<string>Sodogku</string>
	<key>NSUserNotificationsUsageDescription</key>
	<string>We'd like to send you notifications.</string>
	<!--
	  AdMob reads this before any app code runs, which is why the ad unit ids
	  live in shared Kotlin (`AdUnits.kt`) and the *app* id lives here. This
	  is the real id of "Sodogku (iOS)" in the AdMob console (2026-09-21); the
	  units it pairs with are in `AdUnits.IosLive`, and `AdUnits.useTestUnits`
	  decides whether those or Google's samples are requested. A missing key
	  crashes the SDK at start; a wrong one silently serves nothing.
	-->
	<key>GADApplicationIdentifier</key>
	<string>ca-app-pub-7008637445039253~9668136217</string>
	<!--
	  The App Tracking Transparency prompt, `features.md#audience-and-consent`.
	  Shown before the first ad
	  request rather than at launch, so the player is asked at a moment where
	  the answer means something. Apple rejects builds that request the IDFA
	  without this key.
	-->
	<key>NSUserTrackingUsageDescription</key>
	<string>This lets us show ads that are more relevant to you. Sodogku is free because of them.</string>
	<!--
	  The launch screen, which is the first thing anyone sees and was previously
	  nothing at all. With neither this key nor a storyboard, iOS shows a blank
	  system screen and then hands over — the flash of white against the app's
	  cream was the whole of what read as "ugly".

	  Colour only, deliberately. `UIImageName` scales an image to fill the
	  screen, so it cannot place the dog at the offset the first-run screen uses;
	  it would land at a different size on every device and the handoff would
	  jump. Instead the system paints the same cream the app paints, and the dog
	  fades up over it from `SplashScreen.kt` already in its final position. The
	  seam between the system's screen and ours is then invisible, because both
	  sides are the same flat colour.

	  `LaunchBackground` must stay equal to `ColorResource.Cream50` (#F8F2ED).
	  There is no dark variant because the app has one palette.

	  This is also why `INFOPLIST_KEY_UILaunchScreen_Generation` is NO in the
	  project. Left at YES, Xcode synthesises its own empty `UILaunchScreen`
	  dictionary and merges it *inside* the one below, so the built plist ends
	  up with a `UILaunchScreen` nested in a `UILaunchScreen`. iOS still reads
	  the colour, but two sources declaring the same key is the setup where the
	  next edit goes to the wrong one.
	-->
	<key>UILaunchScreen</key>
	<dict>
		<key>UIColorName</key>
		<string>LaunchBackground</string>
	</dict>
	<key>UIBackgroundModes</key>
	<array>
		<string>processing</string>
	</array>
	<!--
	  Custom-scheme deep links. Nothing claims a `sodogku://` URL today: the
	  two that did (`login-callback` and `auth/confirmed`) were the Supabase
	  sign-in return trips, and they went with accounts in C0. The scheme is
	  kept registered, and .onOpenURL in iOSApp.swift still forwards whatever
	  arrives to DeepLinkBridge, so a future link needs a route and not a
	  plist change.

	  Universal Links (https://...) require an `Associated Domains`
	  entitlement (`applinks:example.com`) plus an
	  apple-app-site-association file at https://example.com/.well-known/.
	-->
	<!--
	  The rows we own in the home-screen long-press menu. Everything below them
	  (Delete App, Share App, Edit Home Screen, Require Face ID) is the system's
	  and cannot be removed, reordered or renamed.

	  Static rather than UIApplication.shared.shortcutItems, because a dynamic
	  list does not exist until the app has run once and these two are most
	  wanted by someone who has not opened it in a while. Dynamic items append
	  to these rather than replace them, so the two mechanisms cannot both be
	  used for the same entry.

	  Order is the order shown, and Report a bug is second on purpose: it is the
	  row directly above Delete App.

	  Each ItemType is matched in Kotlin by `AppShortcuts.urlFor`, which turns it
	  into the deep link, and `ShortcutEntriesAgreeTest` fails the build when the
	  two lists of strings drift apart. Nothing here is a URL; the mapping lives
	  in one place and this file names an entry, not a destination.

	  The titles are player-facing English and this app has no translations yet.
	  When it gets them, `UIApplicationShortcutItemTitle` is localized through
	  `InfoPlist.strings` in each `.lproj`, the same mechanism as
	  `CFBundleDisplayName` — it is not reachable from the shared
	  `composeResources` strings, because iOS reads this file before any Kotlin
	  runs.
	-->
	<key>UIApplicationShortcutItems</key>
	<array>
		<dict>
			<key>UIApplicationShortcutItemType</key>
			<string>com.sodogku.shortcut.daily-challenge</string>
			<key>UIApplicationShortcutItemTitle</key>
			<string>Daily challenge</string>
			<key>UIApplicationShortcutItemIconType</key>
			<string>UIApplicationShortcutIconTypePlay</string>
		</dict>
		<dict>
			<key>UIApplicationShortcutItemType</key>
			<string>com.sodogku.shortcut.report-bug</string>
			<key>UIApplicationShortcutItemTitle</key>
			<string>Report a bug</string>
			<key>UIApplicationShortcutItemIconType</key>
			<string>UIApplicationShortcutIconTypeCompose</string>
		</dict>
	</array>
